      "recallAtK": 0.5,
      "reciprocalRank": 0.5,
      "answer": "To turn on automatic reruns in CI for Playwright tests and then inspect what happened in the rerun, you can configure retries in your Playwright configuration file. Here's how you can do it:\n\n1. **Configure retries in the configuration file**:\n   ```js\n   import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';\n\n   export default defineConfig({\n     retries: 3, // Set the number of retries you want\n   });\n   ```\n\n2. **Run the tests with retries enabled**:\n   ```bash\n   npx playwright test\n   ```\n\n3. **Inspect the traces**:\n   - When a test fails and is retried, Playwright will create a trace for each attempt.\n   - Use the [trace viewer](./trace-viewer.md) to open and inspect these traces. The traces are saved locally and can be shared.\n\n4. **View the timeline and inspect DOM snapshots**:\n   - Open the trace viewer and navigate through the timeline.\n   - Use DevTools to inspect DOM snapshots for each action.\n   - Check network requests and other details to understand why the test failed.\n\n5. **Analyze the results**:\n   - Review the logs and traces to identify the root cause of the flakiness.\n   - Adjust your tests or environment to mitigate the issues.\n\nBy following these steps, you can effectively manage flaky tests in CI by rerunning them and analyzing the detailed traces.",
